1
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:11,060
Slide 17 presents "THE TRIAD OF AGENTIC DESIGN: Tasks, Schedules, and Skills." Look at the trinity on screen.

2
00:00:11,960 --> 00:00:28,179
Pillar 1: "Tasks (Objectives)." A task is NOT a procedural script; it is a declarative JSON specification of an end goal—for example, "Reconcile all 12 branch CSV revenue files and report variances over $500."

3
00:00:29,079 --> 00:00:34,445
Pillar 2: "Schedules (Triggers)." This defines when and how the agent wakes up.

4
00:00:34,445 --> 00:00:41,919
It could be a 6:00 AM cron heartbeat, a file upload event in Google Drive, or an incoming webhook from Stripe!

5
00:00:42,819 --> 00:00:48,683
And Pillar 3: "Skills (Capabilities)." The actual hands and tools of the agent!

6
00:00:48,683 --> 00:00:57,889
Modular Python functions decorated with `@tool` that allow the model to query SQL, send Slack alerts, or format Google Docs!

7
00:00:58,789 --> 00:01:05,169
Tasks tell the agent WHAT to achieve, Schedules tell it WHEN to act, and Skills give it the TOOLS to execute!

8
00:01:06,069 --> 00:01:15,429
When Tasks, Schedules, and Skills operate in harmony, your system transitions from passive software to an active autonomous colleague.

